Author-Name: Sempere-Monerris, José J.
Author-Workplace-Name: University of Valencia, Department of Economic Analysis
Author-Name: Vannetelbosch, Vincent J.
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES); UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE)
Title: Bargaining with Externalities: Licensing of an Innovation
Abstract: The objective of this paper is to analyze the relationship between bargaining organizational forms and the licensing of cost-reducing innovations, in order to assess the patent holders optimal policy as well as their welfare properties. Trading mechanisms based on bargaining models with voluntary matching become more relevant when the market for an innovation is small. Our main findings are that when we exclude the use of a reservation price different to the continuation value of the game, it is no longer true that the auction mechanism yields always higher profits to the patentee than a fixed fee. Furthermore, the patent holder prefers the take-it-or-leave-it to the fixed fee, the alternating bids or the simultaneous bids mechanism. Also, bargaining mechanisms allow the patentee to overcome the credibility problem associated with the reservation price. Regarding the social viewpoint (a social agency maximizing the domestic welfare under the assumption that the patentee is a foreign laboratory), it is the best the licensing through the simultaneous bids mechanism. Moreover, licensing through the take-it-or-leave-it is better than licensing through auction mechanisms.
Classification-JEL: C78
Keywords: Bargaining; Matching; Licensing; Innovation; Externalities
Length: 26
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997007
Creation-Date: 1997-05-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/9707.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997007

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Collard, Fabrice
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES); Université de Paris I, MAD
Author-Name: de la Croix, David
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES) ; Belgian National Fund for Scientific Research (FNRS)
Title: Gift exchange and the business cycle: the fair wage strikes back
Abstract: We extend the benchmark RBC model amending the technology for efficiency wage considerations. The disutility of effort depends on current, alternative and past wages. Past wages are treated as the worker's past wages (personal norm case) or as the past wages of the society (social norm case). This last model reproduces the high variability of employment, the low variability of wages and the low wage-employment correlation without requiring a second source of impulsions. Moreover the dynamics of wages and employment is adequately captured when norms adjust slowly to the environment. Efficiency wages are thus useful to solve the business cycle puzzle when we allow for inter-temporal wage comparisons.
Classification-JEL: E24
Keywords: efficiency wage; effort; time-non-separability; RBC; wage sluggishness; business cycle puzzle
Length: 20
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997008
Creation-Date: 1996-12-01
Revision-Date: 1997-04-00
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/9708.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997008

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Vannetelbosch, Vincent J.
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES); UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE)
Title: Profit-Sharing: Does It Reduce Bargaining Inefficiencies ?
Abstract: Within an incomplete information framework, we develop a model of wage determination in a unionized Cournot oligopoly. The assumption of incomplete information allows the possibility of strikes, which waste industry potential ressources, at equilibrium. Facing such deadweight loss, the government or the social planner may decide to adopt a policy, like a profit-sharing scheme. Under two different bargaining structures (firm-level vs industry-level), we investigate the effects of adopting profit-sharing on the wage outcome and the bargaining inefficiencies, like strikes. Our main results are as follows. If the base wage bargaining takes place at the industry-level, then the introduction of a profit-sharing scheme increases the bargaining inefficiencies. But if the base wage bargaining takes place at the firm-level and the number of firms in the industry is greater than two, then the introduction of a profit-sharing scheme reduces the bargaining inefficiencies.
Classification-JEL: C78; J50
Keywords: Wage bargaining; profit-sharing; incomplete information; strikes
Length: 17
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997009
Creation-Date: 1997-06-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/9709.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997009

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: De Vroey, Michel
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Marshall on Equilibrium and Time. A Reconstruction
Abstract: The aim of this paper is to put forward an alternative to the standard interpretation of Marshall's conception of equilibrium and time. In this new interpretation, the market-period receives a central role. It is considered as the unit of time of the analysis, on the one hand, and as the only direct observable market reality of the Marshallian construct, on the other. The standard interpretation of Marshall’s equilibrium taxonomy, with its threefold sub-division (market-period, short-period and long-period equilibrium) is proposed instead. An alternative interpretation of Marshall’s corn model is proposed, wherein perfect information is considered as the linchpin of the making of equilibrium. The relationship between market-period and normal equilibrium is re-examinated. It is shown that market-clearing is always realized in Marshallian theory. Finally, it is argued that this reconstruction permits avoiding a series of interpretative pitfalls.
Length: 27
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997010
Creation-Date: 1997-06-01
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997010

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Grilo, Isabel
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE); Lille III, Gremars
Author-Name: Wauthy, Xavier
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES) ; Belgian National Fund for Scientific Research (FNRS)
Title: Competition through second-hand products when consumers differ in risk aversion
Abstract: When consumers differ in their attitude towards risk, price competition between products of uncertain characteristics may be analyzed using address-models of product differentiation. These models provide a natural set-up for analyzing industries in which products of different reliability may coexist. This is in particular the case of second-hand markets. For such industries, we characterize the Nash equilibrium in prices and the associated market outcomes. They are shown to depend mainly on the distribution of risk aversion in the population. When the degree of reliability is chosen before price competition takes place, maximal differentiation results, yielding either horizontal or vertical differentiation configurations.
Classification-JEL: L13
Keywords: differentiation; risk aversion; risky products
Length: 23
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997011
Creation-Date: 1997-07-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/9711.ps
File-Format: application/postscript
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997011

