Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Bednar, N.
Author-Workplace-Name: Facultés universitaires Saint-Louis, Brussels ; Belgian National Fund for Scientific Research (FNRS)
Author-Name: Monfort, Philippe
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Monetary Policy Coordination, Credibility and Structural Asymmetries
Abstract: The aim of this paper is to discuss the issue of monetary policy coordination in a framework where partners differ not because of idiosyncratic shocks but because of structural asymmetries affecting the conduct of national economic policies. These are introduced at the level of the country size of the economy. We then show that the chances of finding a mutually benefical coordination of the monetary policies which will take place under EMU between countries presenting large structural differences might be a source of tension among the partners for which the adoption of simple convergence rules or of insurance schemes constitutes no remedy.
Length: 27
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997025
Creation-Date: 1997-02-01
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997025

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Germain, Marc
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE)
Author-Name: Magnus, Alphonse
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, Département de Mathématique Appliquée
Title: Consensus versus inflation dans le cadre d'un modèle à la Samuelson élargi : équilibres, cycles et chaos
Abstract: L’objet de ce papier est d’analyser les conséquences de la nature consensuelle ou conflictuelle de la distribution du revenu entre entrepreneurs et salariés sue la dynamique d’un modèle d’inspiration keynésienne. Dans un contexte de concurrence imparfaite, des rigidités de prix et des rationnements quantitatifs peuvent apparaître, de même que des divergences entre valeurs  attendues et effectives. Différents régimes sont caractérisés en fonction de trois critères : les attentes des salariés et des entrepreneurs sont-elles compatibles ? La demande est-elle suffisante en regard de l’offre ? La part des salaires dans la valeur ajoutée satisfait-elle les salariés ? Le modèle se ramène à une équation aux différences du deuxième ordre non analytique. Quatre types d’états à long terme sont identifiés : deux états stationnaires, un cycle et un état chaotique. Seul un des états stationnaires conduit au consensus, les autres étant caractérisés par l’insatisfaction des ménages, qui se traduit par une spirale inflationniste salaire prix. Une condition suffisante sur les paramètres du modèle est énoncée qui assure le consensus.
Length: 28
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997026
Creation-Date: 1997-10-01
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997026

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Laruelle, Annick
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: The EU Decision-Making Procedures : Some Insight from Non Cooperative Game Theory
Abstract: The European Union decision-making process involves three main actors : the Commission, the Council of Ministers and the Parliament. Depending on the issue which is voted upon, there are three possible procedures to make a decision. These are : the proposal procedure, the cooperation procedure and the co-decision procedure. It has often been claimed that the role of the parliament has increased with the cooperation procedure and with the co-decision procedure. The aim of this paper is to verify this assertion. The procedures are modelled as games in extensive form. Simplifying assumptions are made. The set of propositions, amendments and all possible final decisions is represented by the real line. Each player has a preferred position in the real line. Payoff functions are defined as the opposite of the distance from the final decision to the most preferred one. The cost of time is not taken into account. The information is perfect. The games are solved by backward induction. The results show that the introduction of the cooperation procedure has not increased the Parliament’s role. In the co-decision procedure, the parliament’s preference only indirectly influences the final decision via the Conciliation Committee, which has a veto right.
Length: 25
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997027
Creation-Date: 1997-10-01
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:1997027

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Nicolini, Rosella
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Exportation and Foreign Direct Investment in Visegrad Area : An Empirical Analysis
Abstract: The main object of this analysis is to emphasize, empirically, some determinants to exportations and foreign direct investments in Visegrad Area. The interest is to verify whether it is really possible to model location decisions in these countries with proper references already adopted in literature. For exportations, our results are quite similar to the predictions given, in general, by gravity models. However, we have different results for foreign direct investments. This analysis proves that investment decisions, towards Visegrad countries, are substantially influenced by specific (ad-hoc) variables for ex-socialist countries. 
Classification-JEL: F1; F21; L16; L66
Keywords: Bilateral trade; Direct Investment; Gravity Models; Locational determinants; Multinational firms
Length: 32
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 1997028
Creation-Date: 1997-11-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/9728.pdf
