Author-Name: Raouf BOUCEKKINE
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Author-Name: Blanca MARTINEZ
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Author-Name: Cagri SAGLAM
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Technology Adoption, Capital Maintenance and the Technological Gap
Abstract: In this paper, we study the properties of optimal growth models à la Nelson and Phelps (1966) where the labor resources of an economy can be allocated freely either to production, technology adoption or capital maintenance.  We first characterize the balanced growth paths of a benchmark model without maintenance services.  Then we introduce te maintenance activity via the depreciation rate of capital.  We characterize the optimal allocation of labor across the three activities.  We prove that when technolgical shocks occur, equilibrium maintenance and adoption operate in opposite directions.  The main prediction of the model is that though capital maintenance deepens the technological gap by diverting labor resources from adoption, it generally increases the long run output level at equilibrium
Classification-JEL: E22;E32;O40
Keywords: Technology adoption; Capital maintenance; Technological gap; Long run output level
Length: 25
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2001033
Creation-Date: 2001-11-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2001-33.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:2001033

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Aomar IBOURK
Author-Workplace-Name: CREQ, Université de Marrakech - Maroc
Author-Name: Béatrice MAILLARD
Author-Workplace-Name: FLSE, Université catholique de Lille - France
Author-Name: Serge PERELMAN
Author-Workplace-Name: CREPP, Université de Liège
Author-Name: Henri R. SNEESSENS
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES) and IZA, and FLSE, Université catholique de Lille - France
Title: Aggregate Matching Efficiency : A Stochastic Production Frontier Approach, France 1990-1994
Abstract: We evaluate the determinants of aggregate matching efficiency changes through a stochastic Cobb-Douglas production frontier model.  The efficiency coefficient is represented by a stochastic function of variables meant to capture workers and firms characteristics.  The model is estimated on French data covering twenty-two regions from March 1990 till February 1995. Our estimates suggest that aggregate matching efficiency has decreased steadily in the early nineties.  There are also wide cross-regional differences.  On average, about 30% of the variations of efficiency observed across time and regions can be related to changes in the explanatory variables used in the model. The most important explanatory variables are the proportion of youngsters, females and immigrants in the stock of job seekers.  Long-term unemployment has a significant negative effect, population density a significant positive one.  The huge decline in the proportion of permanent job offers has apparently little effect on matching efficiency
Classification-JEL: J64;C24
Keywords: matching efficiency; regional unemployment; stochastic frontier
Length: 25
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2001034
Creation-Date: 2001-11-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2001-34.pdf
