Author-Name: Luisito BERTINELLI
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES) and CORE
Author-Name: Rosella NICOLINI
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES) and CODE, Universitat de Barcelona
Title: Investment decision and the spatial dimension : Evidence from firm level data
Abstract: This paper proposes to investigate the effect of spillovers on location decision of firms.  We develop an analysis merging the geographer toolbox with the standard econometric techniques. For a chosen sample of sectors, through the spatial data analysis, we test the existence of positive spatial autocorrelation for R&D investments that lead R&D expenditure to cluster.  Moreover, we succeed in detecting how far the local environment may influence the firm decisions in R&D investments.  Data confirm tha tthe proximity to other firms investing in R&D may produce positive externalities.  Finally, the diversity vs. specialization debate is tackled.  
Classification-JEL: C23;C25;D21;L20;R12
Keywords: Local clustering;R@D investment;spatial autocorrelation
Length: 30
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2002007
Creation-Date: 2002-02-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2002-7.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:2002007

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Konstantin A. KHOLODILIN
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Unobserved Leading and Coincident Common Factors in the Post-War U.S. Business Cycle
Abstract: The paper introduces a two-factor model of the common leading and coincident economic indicators.  Both factors are unobserved and each of them captures the dynamics of a corresponding group of the observed time series.  The common leading factor is assumed to Granger-cause the common coincident factor. This property is used to estimate these two factors simultaneously and hence more efficiently.  Two models of the latent leading and coincident factors are studied : a model with linear dynamics and a model with Markov-switching dynamics introduced through the leading factor intercept term.  Moreover, a possibility of the individual leading variables having different leads over the common coincident indicator is considered. These models - both with linear and with regime-switching dynamics - were applied to the US monthly macroeconomic time series.  The business cycle dating resulting from the nonlinear model closely corresponds to the NBER chronology and leads its turning points by 3-5 months. 
Classification-JEL: C5;E3
Keywords: dynamic factor analysis;Markov switching;leading indicator;coincident indicator;Granger causality
Length: 21
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2002008
Creation-Date: 2002-01-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2002-8.pdf
