Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Claudio MATTALIA
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES) and Universita degli Studi di Torino - Italy
Title: Information Technologies, Economic Growth and Productivity Shocks
Abstract: This paper develops a multi-sectoral endogenous growth model in order to reproduce some of the essential characteristics of the so-called “ICT Revolution”.  The economy consists of four sectors and the most important features are the embodied nature of technological progress, the horizontal differentiation and the “lab-equipment” specification of R&D.  After the description of the different sectors, the equilibrium conditions are obtained, the balanced growth path is characterized analytically and the corresponding steady state system is derived.  From this system some analytical results can be obtained, in particular it turns out that shocks on the productivity of the different sectors have permanent effects on long-term growth (contrary to the version of the model without the “lab-equipment” assumption, where only a shock on the productivity of the R&D sector influences long-term growth).  These results are confirmed, in the last part of the paper, by the numerical simulation of the model, that allows also to analyse the short-run response of the system to the different shocks that can hit the economy and to study the robustness of the model.  
Classification-JEL: E22;O40;C63
Keywords: Information Technology, Endogenous Growth
Length: 39
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2002026
Creation-Date: 2002-09-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2002-26.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:2002026

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Konstantin, KHOLODILIN
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Two Alternative Approaches to Modelling the Nonlinear Dynamics of the Composite Economic Indicator
Abstract: The analysis and prediction of the short-run economic dynamics, or the evolution of the business cycle, often require a construction of the composite economic indicator (CEI).  This indicator may be endowed with nonlinear dynamics to take care of the possible asymmetries between different phases of the business cycle.  This paper suggests using the smooth transition autoregression to model the CEI.  The performance of this model is compared to the already classical CEI with regime switching.  Both models turn out to produce statistically equally good results in terms of forecasting the business cycle turning points.  
Classification-JEL: C4;C5;E3
Keywords: composite economic indicator, Markov switching, smooth transition autoregression, turning points, NBER dating, forecasting
Length: 21
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
