Author-Name: Klaus WAELDE
Author-Workplace-Name: University o Dresden - Germany
Title: Capital accumulation in a model of growth and creative destruction
Abstract: Capital accumulation and creative destruction is modeled together with risk-averse households. The novel aspect - risk-averse households - allows to use well-known models not only for analyzing long-run growth as in the literature but also short-run fluctuations.  The model remains analytically tractable due to a very convenient property of the household’s investment decision in this stochastic continuous-time setup.
Classification-JEL: E32;O41;O31
Keywords: Creative destruction;Risk averse households;Capital accumulation;Endogenous fluctuations and growth
Length: 19
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2002038
Creation-Date: 2002-10-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2002-38.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:2002038

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Bruno CRUZ
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Author-Name: Aude POMMERET
Author-Workplace-Name: DEEP-HEC - Lausanne University
Title: Does public investment reduce private investment risk ?  A real option approach
Abstract: In this paper, the public investment provision takes place in a stochastic environnement.  The role of the government is to remove a part of the uncertainty faced by the firm.  If the government simply maximizes the value of the firm, then the optimal tax is smaller under imperfect competition then it is under perfect competition since more public capital reduces the selling price.  But if the government seeks to maximize the consumer surplus, tax and public capital provision are a mean to correct the market and the optimal tax is then higher
Classification-JEL: E22;E62;H41
Keywords: irreversible investment;public capital;uncertainty
Length: 26
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2002039
Creation-Date: 2002-11-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2002-39.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvir:2002039

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Mathias HUJGERBUELHER
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Tax Progression and Human Capital in a Matching Framework
Abstract: This paper investigates the effect of tax progression on labour market outcomes in an equilibrium search model with wage bargain and endogenous human capital. We show that this effect depends on whether the firm and the worker can write a binding contract on human capital investments or not.  If complete contracts are not possible, either the firm or the worker invests in human capital.  We find that the effect of tax progression on human capital depends crucially on which party invests and the tax function that is considered.  When the firms invests, we cannot exclude that a higher tax progression increases human capital. Moreover, we find that when a complete contract is possible or when the firms invests, the optimal tax rate in a model with human capital is at least as high as in a model without human capital. 
Classification-JEL: H22;J24;J41
Keywords: Tax progression;Human Capital;Search
Length: 44
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, IRES
Number: 2002040
Creation-Date: 2002-11-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2002-40.pdf
